  "notes": "West leads the ♣K against 7♠. After winning the opening club lead, South has 3 losers (2 diamonds and 1 club) to dispose of. Dummy's heart winners will take care of 2 of those losers. The diamond finesse is a possible solution to South's third loser.\n\nThe odds of the diamond finesse working are about 50%. South can get better odds with careful timing as long as spades are no worse than 3-1. South should start by drawing 2 rounds of trump (making sure to save the ♠2), leaving 1 trump outstanding if trumps are 3-1.\n\nThe ♥A is now cashed. Dummy is entered in diamonds and dummy's hearts are cashed for 2 diamond discards. North's other top diamond honour is now cashed. When both opponents follow, the contract is assured.\n\nA diamond from North is ruffed high, establishing a winner for South's club loser. The ♠2 can be played to the dummy (drawing the last trump) as an entry to the good diamond.\n\nThe contract will make whenever diamonds are 3-2 (as well as on some bad diamond breaks). The odds of a 3-2 break are about 68%. Playing the hand as suggested is thus superior to taking the 50% diamond finesse.",
